/**
\defgroup distrib_core The structure of a distribution
\brief Common interface, parameter and kink descriptors, packed symmetric tensors, and tensor error scaling for univariate distributions.

Include `<laplace/distrib/core.hpp>`; every type, constant, and helper lives in `laplace::distrib`. Functions in this module are marked `LAPLACE_HD` and can be called on the host and inside Kokkos kernels on the device.

A distribution in Laplace (a distrib) is a lightweight C++ structure whose non-static fields are its natural parameters \f$\theta\f$. Distributions are constructed per observation and copied by value into kernels, where statistical quantities are evaluated through `const` member functions with separate functions for parameter derivatives up to order 6. The enumeration \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_kind "distrib_kind" distinguishes `continuous` distributions, which provide `logpdf()`, `quantile()`, and continuous cumulative functions on \f$\mathbb{R}\f$, from `discrete` distributions, which provide `logpmf()` and discrete cumulative functions on integer observations. The combinatorial indexing helpers in this header are exact integer routines, and the accuracy of distribution derivative tensors is measured against high-precision references in ulps of each order's largest component (see \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured").

## Distribution interface

Every distribution provides static metadata, log-density or log-mass evaluations, support bounds, quadrature hints, and kink queries. Required members must be defined on every distribution struct, with observation-derivative members required only for `distrib_kind::continuous`. Specialized cumulative functions, quantiles, and expected Fisher information are optional: when a distribution omits `expected_info()`, `expected_info_d1()` through `expected_info_d4()`, discrete cumulative functions, or quantiles, the compile-time dispatchers in \ref distrib_generic fall back to generic numerical routes.

| Member | Return type | Requirement | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `kind` | `distrib_kind` | Required | Distribution kind (`continuous` or `discrete`). |
| `n_params` | `int` | Required | Number of natural parameters \f$P\f$. |
| `params` | `const distrib_param_info[P]` | Required | Descriptors of each natural parameter. |
| `logpdf(y)` / `logpmf(y)` | `double` | Required | Log-density \f$\log f(y; \theta)\f$ or log-mass \f$\log p(y; \theta)\f$. |
| `logpdf_d1(y)` ... `_d6(y)` | `sym_tensor<P, K>` | Required | Parameter derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^K}{\partial\theta^K} \log f(y; \theta)\f$ for \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$. |
| `logpmf_d1(y)` ... `_d6(y)` | `sym_tensor<P, K>` | Required | Parameter derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^K}{\partial\theta^K} \log p(y; \theta)\f$ for \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$. |
| `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` | `sym_tensor<P, K>` | Continuous | Mixed partial derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^{J+K}}{\partial y^J \partial\theta^K} \log f(y; \theta)\f$ for \f$J + K \le 6\f$. |
| `support_lower()` | `double` | Required | Lower limit of the support at \f$\theta\f$. |
| `support_upper()` | `double` | Required | Upper limit of the support at \f$\theta\f$. |
| `center()` | `double` | Required | Rough central location, used to place quadrature nodes. |
| `scale()` | `double` | Required | Rough spread, used to size quadrature panels. |
| `param_kinks(j, y)` | `distrib_kinks` | Required | Non-smooth points in parameter \f$\theta_j\f$ at observation \f$y\f$. |
| `y_regularity` | `int` | Continuous | Differentiability order of \f$\log f\f$ in \f$y\f$ (`infinitely_smooth` or non-negative integer). |
| `y_kinks()` | `distrib_kinks` | Continuous | Non-smooth points in \f$y\f$ at parameters \f$\theta\f$ (optional in \ref distrib_generic). |
| `expected_info()` | `sym_tensor<P, 2>` | Optional | Expected Fisher information \f$I(\theta) = -\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^2} \log f(Y; \theta)\right]\f$. |
| `expected_info_d1()` ... `_d4()` | `expected_info_tensor<P, K>` | Optional | Parameter derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^K}{\partial\theta^K} I_{ab}(\theta)\f$ for \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 4\}\f$. |
| `cdf(y)` | `double` | Optional | Cumulative distribution function \f$F(y) = \mathbb{P}(Y \le y)\f$. |
| `ccdf(y)` | `double` | Optional | Complementary cumulative distribution function \f$1 - F(y) = \mathbb{P}(Y > y)\f$. |
| `log_cdf(y)` | `double` | Optional | Logarithm of the cumulative distribution function, \f$\log F(y)\f$. |
| `log_ccdf(y)` | `double` | Optional | Logarithm of the complementary cumulative distribution function, \f$\log(1 - F(y))\f$. |
| `log_cdf_d1(y)` ... `_d6(y)` | `sym_tensor<P, K>` | Optional | Parameter derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^K}{\partial\theta^K} \log F(y; \theta)\f$ for \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$. |
| `log_ccdf_d1(y)` ... `_d6(y)` | `sym_tensor<P, K>` | Optional | Parameter derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^K}{\partial\theta^K} \log(1 - F(y; \theta))\f$ for \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$. |
| `quantile(p)` | `double` | Optional | Quantile function \f$F^{-1}(p)\f$ for \f$p \in (0, 1)\f$. |

## Parameter and kink descriptors

The structure \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_param_info "distrib_param_info" records the analytical properties of each parameter. Field `name` holds the parameter identifier (such as `"mu"`, `"sigma"`, or `"phi"`), `lower` and `upper` define the open natural domain from `lower` to `upper` with infinite limits represented by \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_infinity "distrib_infinity", `circular` flags periodic domains, and `moves_support` indicates whether varying the parameter shifts the support endpoints. Smoothness in that parameter is given by `regularity` for \f$\log f(y; \theta)\f$ and by `cdf_regularity` for \f$\log F(y; \theta)\f$ and \f$\log(1 - F(y; \theta))\f$: each holds \ref laplace::distrib::infinitely_smooth "infinitely_smooth" when infinitely differentiable, or a non-negative integer \f$r\f$ when \f$C^r\f$ with kinks. Continuous distributions also declare `y_regularity` for the smoothness of \f$\log f\f$ in \f$y\f$.

A \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_kinks "distrib_kinks" struct stores up to two coordinates in `point[2]` and their count in `count`, together with `continuous_order`, the highest derivative order that remains continuous across the kink. At observation \f$y\f$, `param_kinks(j, y)` returns non-smooth points in \f$\theta_j\f$, and `y_kinks()` returns non-smooth points in \f$y\f$ at the current parameters \f$\theta\f$.

Smooth distributions such as \ref laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib "gaussian1_distrib" and \ref laplace::distrib::gamma1_distrib "gamma1_distrib" report `infinitely_smooth` for every parameter and for `y_regularity`. In \ref laplace::distrib::laplace1_distrib "laplace1_distrib", the location parameter \f$\mu\f$ has `regularity = 0`, `y_regularity = 0`, and `cdf_regularity = 1` at the kink \f$\mu = y\f$. Because the pointwise second derivative in \f$\mu\f$ vanishes almost everywhere and omits a point mass at the kink, the expected Fisher information is obtained from the score variance (Lehmann and Casella, 1998) rather than \f$-\mathbb{E}[\partial^2 \log f / \partial\mu^2]\f$, while \f$\log F\f$ integrates the density and is one order smoother.

## Symmetric tensors and combinatorial helpers

Derivatives of order \f$K\f$ with respect to \f$P\f$ parameters are symmetric tensors stored in \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor "sym_tensor", which packs only unique components in an array `v`. The element count is computed by \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor_size "sym_tensor_size()":
\f[
\mathrm{size}(P, K) = \binom{P + K - 1}{K}.
\f]
This count delegates to \ref laplace::distrib::binomial_coef "binomial_coef()", which evaluates \f$\binom{n}{k}\f$ for \f$n \ge 0\f$ and returns `0` when \f$k < 0\f$ or \f$k > n\f$.

Components are ordered lexicographically by sorted multi-indices \f$0 \le i_0 \le i_1 \le \dots \le i_{K-1} < P\f$. The linear index of a multi-index is computed by \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor_position "sym_tensor_position()":
\f[
\mathrm{pos}(i_0, \dots, i_{K-1}) = \sum_{j=0}^{K-1} \sum_{v=\mathrm{prev}}^{i_j - 1} \binom{P - v + K - j - 2}{K - j - 1},
\f]
where \f$\mathrm{prev} = 0\f$ for \f$j = 0\f$ and \f$\mathrm{prev} = i_{j-1}\f$ for \f$j > 0\f$. For \f$K = 0\f$, `sym_tensor<P, 0>` has size 1 with the single scalar stored at `v[0]`. For any two-parameter distribution (\f$P = 2\f$) and order \f$K \ge 0\f$, the component with \f$b\f$ derivatives in \f$\theta_1\f$ and \f$K - b\f$ derivatives in \f$\theta_0\f$ sits at `v[b]`. For example, with \f$P = 2\f$ and \f$K = 3\f$, there are \f$\binom{4}{3} = 4\f$ components: `(0, 0, 0)` at `v[0]`, `(0, 0, 1)` at `v[1]`, `(0, 1, 1)` at `v[2]`, and `(1, 1, 1)` at `v[3]`.

For continuous distributions, `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` evaluates the mixed partial derivatives \f$\frac{\partial^{J+K}}{\partial y^J \partial\theta^K} \log f(y; \theta)\f$ for total order \f$J + K \le 6\f$, returning a `sym_tensor<P, K>` packed over \f$\theta\f$ as above. Each pair \f$(J, K)\f$ is an independent template instantiation evaluated only when called. Calling \f$J = 0, K = 0\f$ returns the log-density in `v[0]`, and \f$J = 0, K \ge 1\f$ matches `logpdf_dk<K>(y)`; discrete distributions do not provide derivatives in \f$y\f$.

The expected Fisher information matrix \f$I(\theta) = -\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^2}\log f\right]\f$ is symmetric positive definite of size \f$P \times P\f$, so it is stored as `sym_tensor<P, 2>` over the \f$\binom{P + 1}{2}\f$ pairs \f$0 \le a \le b < P\f$. Its parameter derivatives of order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 4\}\f$ are held by \ref laplace::distrib::expected_info_tensor "expected_info_tensor", which stores `sym_tensor<P, K> pair[n_pairs]`. For each pair \f$a \le b\f$ in `sym_tensor<P, 2>` order, the corresponding entry of `pair` holds the order-\f$K\f$ derivative tensor of \f$I_{ab}(\theta)\f$, giving log-likelihood expectation derivatives up to total order 6.

## Accuracy and tensor error scaling

Scalar distribution functions are measured in units in the last place (ulp) against double-double reference values \f$(hi, lo)\f$ (Dekker, 1971), as described on \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured". For a symmetric derivative tensor \f$v \in \mathbb{R}^M\f$ of order \f$K\f$, individual components frequently cross zero at different points across the parameter space, causing plain relative ulp error to diverge even when the absolute error is near machine epsilon.

To prevent artificial error inflation at zeros of sub-dominant components, Laplace measures tensor accuracy on the conditioned scale \f$S\f$ (see \ref accuracy_conditioned) given by the largest reference component of that order at the evaluation point:
\f[
S = \max_{0 \le i < M} |hi_i|, \qquad \mathrm{error}_{\mathrm{scaled}} = \max_{0 \le i < M} \frac{|(v_i - hi_i) - lo_i|}{\mathrm{ulp}(S)}.
\f]
This reports the error of every component in ulps of the dominant component of the order.

## Kernel execution example

The following example constructs a `gaussian1_distrib` inside a Kokkos parallel loop and reads its log-density, score, and expected information:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/distrib/gaussian1.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

void evaluate_gaussian_kernel(Kokkos::View<const double*> y,
                              Kokkos::View<double*> log_lik,
                              Kokkos::View<double*> score_mu,
                              Kokkos::View<double*> info_mu,
                              double mu_val, double sigma_val) {
  const int n = y.extent(0);
  Kokkos::parallel_for("eval_gaussian", n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    // Construct distribution for observation
    const laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib dist{mu_val, sigma_val};

    // Evaluate log-density
    log_lik(i) = dist.logpdf(y(i));

    // Evaluate first derivative in parameters: v[0] is d/dmu, v[1] is d/dsigma
    const auto score = dist.logpdf_d1(y(i));
    score_mu(i) = score[0];

    // Evaluate expected information: pair[0] is (mu, mu)
    const auto info = dist.expected_info();
    info_mu(i) = info[0];
  });
}
\endcode

## Module types and functions

In `laplace::distrib`, the enumeration \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_kind "distrib_kind" distinguishes `continuous` and `discrete` distributions, \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_param_info "distrib_param_info" describes natural parameter properties, and \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_kinks "distrib_kinks" records non-smooth points and continuity orders. Packed derivative storage is provided by \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor "sym_tensor" and \ref laplace::distrib::expected_info_tensor "expected_info_tensor", using the constants \ref laplace::distrib::distrib_infinity "distrib_infinity" for infinite bounds and \ref laplace::distrib::infinitely_smooth "infinitely_smooth" for \f$C^\infty\f$ regularity. Tensor sizing and indexing are computed by \ref laplace::distrib::binomial_coef "binomial_coef()", \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor_size "sym_tensor_size()", and \ref laplace::distrib::sym_tensor_position "sym_tensor_position()".

## References

- T. J. Dekker (1971), A floating-point technique for extending the available precision, Numerische Mathematik, 18(3), 224–242. https://doi.org/10.1007/bf01397083
- E. L. Lehmann and G. Casella (1998), Theory of Point Estimation, 2nd edition, Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/b98854
*/